Subject: [PATCH 0011] Run the adaptive interpreter without specialization where a pointer is a capability
Upstream: CPython 3.13.7
Upstream-Archive: https://www.python.org/ftp/python/3.13.7/Python-3.13.7.tgz
Upstream-SHA256: 6c9d80839cfa20024f34d9a6dd31ae2a9cd97ff5e980e969209746037a5153b2
Build-Variants: capstone64 domain (musl)
Apply-From: upstream source root, with patch --batch --forward --fuzz=0 -p1

Specialized instructions cache PyObject pointers in the instruction stream
(write_obj/read_obj in pycore_code.h): a memcpy into 16-bit code units, at
2-byte alignment. A capability cannot survive that. The store keeps the
address and not the tag, and the pointer read back is an integer that traps
when used. On the first boot that reached bytecode, the interpreter halted in
_PyEval_EvalFrameDefault (cause 24, "Cap mem access requires capability") at
the Py_INCREF of an object it had just read with a 16-byte memcpy from a
2-byte-aligned address in the code array -- read_obj's shape; which opcode it
was is not established.

ENABLE_SPECIALIZATION is 0 there, as it already is in the free-threaded build:
instructions stay adaptive and unspecialized, and nothing writes an object
into a cache. The cost is speed, not behaviour.

--- a/Include/internal/pycore_code.h
+++ b/Include/internal/pycore_code.h
@@ -314,6 +314,14 @@
 #ifdef Py_GIL_DISABLED
 // gh-115999 tracks progress on addressing this.
 #define ENABLE_SPECIALIZATION 0
+#elif _Py_SIZEOF_ADDRESS < SIZEOF_VOID_P
+// A pointer is a capability here. Specialized instructions keep PyObject
+// pointers in their inline caches (write_obj/read_obj), which are 16-bit code
+// units: a 2-byte-aligned memcpy there keeps the address and drops the tag, and
+// the pointer read back traps when used. Measured: the interpreter halted at
+// the Py_INCREF of an object just read out of a cache that way. The adaptive
+// instructions run unspecialized instead, as in the free-threaded build.
+#define ENABLE_SPECIALIZATION 0
 #else
 #define ENABLE_SPECIALIZATION 1
 #endif
